// anchor: array-literal-index-target
// An integer array literal built and then read back by index. Passing under the
// VM is not evidence the native backend accepts it: the VM allocates and indexes
// a container at run time, while the native backend demands a frozen storage
// fact for every value it emits, and before this case no family stated one for
// an array allocation. Each line below pins a separate fact the two backends
// have to agree on:
//   - the allocation's storage is the owned tagged outer value, stated by its
//     own frozen family; without that family the AOT build refuses the whole
//     module rather than emitting the allocation;
//   - the Xi representation choice for the container operand of an index access
//     has to name that same tagged storage, or the backend materializes a
//     native pointer view the frozen plan has no adapter row for and the module
//     is refused again;
//   - the index and the element keep their own native scalar storage, so the
//     element read still crosses a representation boundary that the plan states
//     as an ordinary integer box.
// The authority stops at a signed 64-bit element on purpose: a reference
// capable element would leave a reference-count obligation on every element
// store that no frozen row here describes.
fn main() {
    var a = [1, 2, 3]
    print(a[0])
    print(a[2])

    a[1] = 99
    print(a[1])

    var negatives = [-7, 11]
    print(negatives[0] + negatives[1])

    var picked = a[1]
    print(picked)

    var i = 0
    var total = 0
    while (i < 3) {
        total = total + a[i]
        i = i + 1
    }
    print(total)
}

main()
